aggregate — a todo of todos. the global index computes; it does not collect.

a project keeps its own todo in its own place (its todo.html, or a page of
its own). the global page holds links to those places, and standalone
items. a card's tally is read from the page there, never typed here:
page/todo's states() and tally() — a folder's own index.html or todo.html,
or the page a link names. a borrowed link (a file symlinked in) is not the
folder's own page.

  typed at rest, derived in motion
    a note's state is typed: a person moves it by editing (renderer)
    a link's state is derived: the page it names holds the nodes
    a run's state is derived: ls is the status

todo and pipeline are one structure with two evaluators. the evaluator
differs — you, or the runner — the tree and the renderer do not. a gate is
an open node, reads are after:. "open is a decision for a human" holds at
every level.

the lifecycle of a node: a bare name (an idea) -> a line -> a README -> a
folder -> a folder with its own todo. the same node at every size; only the
tally changes. an idea lives in the global list alone until it is work;
then the content moves to its place and the entry becomes a link.

a project's todo can be a folder, todo/, an entry a task. a folder named
todo writes its page, todo.html, beside itself, unless it has an
index.html of its own.

the isomorphism: the global page is the forest. a card folded shows the
tally; open, it holds the project's real task nodes, inlined from its
todo/, ids the path down (index.html#proj/task). after: links resolve
inside the card. the engine has one rule for it: one open is the page,
and j k walk it -- into the node's nodes where it has them, and stepping
off its end folds it and moves on. run.mjs checks it: step in for a node
with nodes, come out for a leaf.

the lens rule: the todo page is the todo lens only. a folder's file listing
is the explorer's lens (the shelf) and is not shown; a card holds state,
text and its tree. a link to a page keeps its link, the link being the
entry itself.

one list, every page (renderer) is what makes the fold cheap: the tally is
a query over the list. open: scan-and-include (a folder with a todo page
joins by itself) vs the link, curated and ordered, which the page uses.

See renderer (the list), markup (the note's form), studio (the whole).
